        customers: ["**IKEA**, a customer for 27 years and 57% of FY26 revenue; CRISIL notes that no single product is over 15% of its IKEA sales. IKEA and Indian sales are billed in rupees.", "**Tesco (17 years) and Asda (14 years)** in the UK, Michaels and other US retailers; a third customer grew about 225% in FY26, and management hints at a large new US programme.", "**Indian shoppers** through modern retail and its own All Time brand; India was 16–17% of revenue."],
        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "Injection moulding is common, but European retailers demand in-house mould design, all-electric machines, more than 25% recycled plastic and ethical-sourcing certificates (Higg, EcoVadis, amfori, ISCC) before they hand over a long programme. Few Indian moulders meet those standards at its scale." },
        moat: "Decades-long programmes, its own mould design and the certifications retailers require make it costly to replace, and IKEA passes on plastic prices by formula. The weakness is that IKEA's weight gives the customer the upper hand on price and volume.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Filling the plant**: capacity reached 41,000 tonnes after the listing-funded expansion, and use rose from 52% in Q4 FY26 to 65% in Q1 FY27. Each 10 points of use is about ₹100cr of sales; at 75% it could sell about ₹800cr, some 30% above FY26, without new capex.", "**Retailers buying outside China**: new programmes and customers, especially in the US (19% of Q1 sales); management speaks of an opportunity that ‘would materially change the shape of our geography’ if it converts.", "**IKEA's own volumes**: at 57% of sales, IKEA's range changes and sales decide much of the year.", "**Own brand and bamboo**: the All Time brand is to grow to 22–25% of sales, and the bamboo plant could add ₹60cr at full use.", "**Guidance and capex**: FY27 revenue growth of 15–20%; 14 new machines (+1,500 tonnes) for Q4 FY27 and about 4,000 tonnes more to be ordered in Q3 once customer programmes are fixed."],
        inputs: "**Plastic granules (polypropylene and other polymers)**, more than 25% of them recycled, plus packaging, electricity for the all-electric machines, and steel for moulds. By management's measure raw material is 52–53% of product cost; on the filed figures materials were 61% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Passed on by formula, with an eight-week lag.** IKEA's contract passes plastic prices through about eight weeks late; in India the company halted supply for about eight weeks in Q1 FY27 to force a full price rise, and on 15–20% of revenue only half the rise was passed on. A 40% rise in plastic needs about a 20% price rise. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 6 points (15% to 9%). **What happened:** India's polypropylene price index was 100 in Q1 FY25 and stayed between 93 and 102 until Q4 FY26, then jumped to 119 in Q1 FY27 (management says plastic prices rose 40–50% at the peak). The margin fell from 19.2% to 14.2%, but most of that fall, including a low of 10.9% in Q2 FY26, came while plastic was flat: the new plant added costs (staff up 24%) before it had orders to fill it.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Chinese and other Asian moulders', note: 'Supply the same global retailers; their share is what All Time wins as retailers look for a second source outside China.' }, { name: 'Cello, Milton and other Indian brands', note: 'Far larger in Indian shops, where its own All Time brand is small.' }],
        position: "**India's largest exporter of plastic houseware and plastic furniture**, selling to 29 countries, and a long-standing IKEA supplier.",
        money: "Screener shows net cash of **₹40cr**: borrowings fell to ₹86cr at March 2026 from ₹223cr after ₹143cr of listing money repaid debt. Of ₹113.7cr raised for new machines and a warehouse, only ₹23.3cr had been spent by March, and ₹90.4cr sits in deposits. Further capex is modest (+1,500 tonnes in Q4 FY27, bamboo ₹15cr). CRISIL rates it in the A category, with a downgrade trigger if the margin stays below 14%. Three promoter-directors and relatives drew about ₹7.3cr in FY26 against a profit of ₹35.6cr. Promoters hold **70.2%**, unchanged since the listing.",
        watch: ["Plant use above 75% and a margin above 16% in H2 FY27.", "A signed large US programme.", "The ₹90cr of unspent listing money put to work."],
