        moat: "‘Borosil’ is the name Indians use for all heat-proof glass, and it owns the only local borosilicate furnace, likely soon to be protected by an anti-dumping duty. The weakness is that the brand has not produced good returns: prices lag costs by one to two quarters and Chinese dumping has capped them.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Anti-dumping duty**: on 24 September 2026 the Directorate General of Trade Remedies (DGTR) recommended a five-year duty on Chinese borosilicate glassware, finding that dumping had ‘materially retarded’ the Indian industry; the Finance Ministry has yet to notify it.", "**Import rules**: quality-control orders from the Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) now bar uncertified steel bottles and, from 1 October 2026, some appliances. They cost it about ₹100cr of bottle sales in FY26 but now favour its own certified plant.", "**New capacity**: the bottle plant's third line in Q2 FY27; a ₹42cr glassware plant at Bharuch by end-Q3 FY27 that brings in-house about ₹100cr of sales now bought from Borosil Scientific; and a ₹50cr furnace rebuild lifting borosilicate output from 25 to 32 tonnes a day by FY28.", "**Opalware**: grew about 9% in Q1 on volume, and management now expects about the same pace from here; in May it had expected ‘muted’ growth for about a year as rivals added capacity.", "**Guidance**: revenue growth of 15–20% a year in the medium term and an FY27 margin of ‘about 18%’, a target the new chief executive re-explained three times on one call; Q1 was 14.2%."],
        inputs: "**Glass-making materials** (silica sand, plus boron compounds for borosilicate glass) melted in **gas-fired furnaces**, and **finished goods bought in**: steel bottles, appliances and cookware, plus about ₹100cr of glassware from Borosil Scientific. Materials were 40% of sales in FY26, and power and factory costs a high 17%, because the furnaces burn gas round the clock.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on, slowly.** The swing cost is gas, not materials: fuel is normally about 4% of sales in opalware and 5.5% in glassware, but it rose from 2.9% to 5.8% of sales in Q1 FY27, a net hit of about ₹10cr. Price rises of 5–7% (8–10% in some items), effective from mid-May, recovered only about ₹2cr of a ₹12cr cost rise in the quarter, and Chinese imports cap how far it can go. On bought-in bottles and appliances it earns only a trading margin, which is why its own bottle plant should lift that gross margin by at least 10 points after six to twelve months of settling in. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 4 points (14% to 10%). **What happened:** there is no Indian price index for its glass-and-gas mix, so there is no chart line. The filed margin went from 15% in FY24 to 16% in FY25 and 14% in FY26, falling to 11% in Q4 FY26 and 14% in Q1 FY27 as its gas bill rose about two-and-a-half times at the peak and bottle sales were lost. Solar plants now supply 61% of its power and should save about ₹28cr in FY27.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'Chinese imports', note: 'Borosilicate glassware sold below cost, according to the trade-remedies finding; brands that source from China undercut Borosil in shops.' }, { name: 'La Opala and Cello World', note: 'Rivals in opal tableware and glassware; rivals have added opal capacity, though Borosil’s opalware still grew about 9% in Q1.' }, { name: 'Milton and Cello World', note: 'Larger rivals in insulated steel bottles.' }],
        position: "**India's only borosilicate kitchen-glass maker** (a market of about ₹415cr), with **about a third of opalware** (about ₹1,350cr) through Larah and a smaller share of steel bottles (a market of over ₹2,000cr); the market sizes are company estimates.",
        money: "Screener shows net debt of **₹62cr**; the company put it at ₹50cr in March and ₹99cr in June 2026 (debt ₹155cr, cash ₹56cr). FY27 capex was raised to ₹125–150cr from ₹110cr, paid from cash and loans; free cash has been negative for four straight years, with 282 days of stock. It raised ₹150cr through a share placement (QIP) in June 2024. Promoters hold **64.7%**, down from 70.2% in September 2023 after the share placement.",
