        moat: "Low-cost in-house manufacturing and wide online reach make it a volume leader in value cookers and induction cooktops. The weakness is a value brand with no switching cost in a crowded market: it calls itself ‘cost plus’, and ICRA says competition constrains its pricing.",
        salesDrivers: ["**The induction rush**: induction sales rose 316% in Q1 FY27; management expects induction revenue to double in FY27 to at least 20% of sales, but analysts on the call doubted it will last once cooking gas is plentiful again.", "**Operating leverage**: plants ‘designed for ₹3,000cr’ against ₹1,605cr of FY26 sales, so sales can grow without big spending; management targets 15–20% growth a year and a 14–15% margin in two to three years.", "**Stores and shops**: 25–30 new stores a quarter, and general trade grew 56% in Q1, its best in three years.", "**Exports**: IKEA from Q2 FY27, a quarter late; in August management would give no absolute figure (in May it aimed at ₹40–50cr in FY27), only that exports including IKEA should reach about 15% of revenue within two years.", "**Capex**: the FY27 budget is about ₹40cr, but new cooker, tri-ply, induction and appliance lines have since been added, and FY26 capex ran to ₹109–111cr against a ₹50cr plan."],
        inputs: "**Aluminium** for cookers and cookware, **stainless steel** and **cast iron**, **copper and electronic parts** for induction cooktops and appliances (the imported glass tops and magnets are 33–40% of an induction cooktop's input value), and packaging. Materials were 61% of sales in FY26, the joint-highest share in the group.",
        costEffect: "**Passed on, at cost plus.** Management calls the company ‘cost plus’: it resets prices every quarter when metal costs move, which works because rivals face the same costs. It raised prices about 10% in Q1 FY27 and still lifted its gross margin by 1.3 points, to 39.6%. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 6 points (10% to 4%). **What happened:** India's aluminium price index rose from 100 in Q1 FY24 to 159 in Q1 FY27, most of it in the last year, while the margin rose from 8.1% to 11.2%. Prices kept up with metal; the margin moved with volume, dipping to 8–9% in slack quarters (7.7% at the low) and reaching 11–12% when its plants were busy. Below that line, depreciation (₹80cr) and interest (₹30cr) took most of FY26's ₹157cr operating profit.",
